---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const bmiHref = toolPathBySlug('bmi-calculator');
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>How do you read a waist to hip ratio? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Divide the waist by the hip. An 80 cm waist and a 100 cm hip give 0.80.
      Reading the sex-specific WHO band from a waist and a hip is what the second card does.
    </p>
    <p>
      For men the substantially increased cut is 0.90. For women it is 0.85.
      Checking a 0.80 ratio against the male cut of 0.90 reads Below 0.90 on the band.
      The mixed-unit note stays hidden on that pair, because both tapes are centimetres.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Waist to Hip Ratio Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-whr-is">
    <h2>What is waist to hip ratio? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Waist to hip ratio is one circumference divided by the other. The result has no unit when both tapes match.
      The WHO expert consultation of 8 to 11 December 2008 published the cuts used here.
    </p>
    <p>
      A man at 95 cm over 100 cm reads 0.95, which is at or above 0.90.
      A woman at 32 inches over 40 inches reads 0.80, which is below 0.85.
      The band names the cut. It does not add a second measurement.
    </p>
    <p>
      The formula is waist divided by hip. A whr calculator applies the WHO 2008 cut to that ratio.
      For example, men and women do not share one number.
      Input: an 80 cm waist and a 100 cm hip, both in centimetres.
    </p>
    <p>
      Output: 0.80, which sits below the male cut of 0.90.
      What happens with one circumference in inches is a unit mismatch, not a second formula.
      Waist hip ratio risk is that band. A central obesity calculator would claim more, and this page does not score central obesity risk.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="not-height">
    <h2>What is the difference between waist to hip and waist to height? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The difference between waist to hip ratio and waist to height ratio is the second tape.
      This page divides waist by hip. Height is not an input, so waist to height is not computed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Expecting a waist to height ratio from this page sends you looking for a number the form cannot make.
      BMI from the same body is a different question, on the <a href={bmiHref}>BMI calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mixed-units">
    <h2>What if the two tapes use different units? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Catching a waist in inches beside a hip in centimetres is the note on this page.
      A 32 cm waist over a 102 cm hip is 0.31, which is outside the range a real pair usually has.
      If that waist was inches, the ratio is about 0.80, and the note says inches.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both sides wrong in the same way stay quiet. 32 and 40, both read as centimetres, are still 0.80.
      The ratio cannot see a unit that both numbers share. Only a mismatch shows up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes">
    <h2>Which waist to hip mistakes are easy? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Mixed units">
      <p>
        Typing one circumference in inches and the other in centimetres still produces a ratio.
        The note appears when fixing one implausible side lands back in a plausible band.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="A cut read as a diagnosis">
      <p>
        Treating the ratio as a diagnosis of central obesity overreads a screening cut from 2008.
        The page does not diagnose, and it does not estimate body fat.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="The other ratio">
      <p>
        Expecting a waist to height ratio from this page will not work. Height is absent.
        Waist divided by hip is the only figure in the hero.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Same unit on both tapes">
      <p>
        Keep both circumferences in centimetres, or both in inches.
        The number is dimensionless only when that is true.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="A screen, on your device">
      <p>
        Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded.
        Tape at the narrowest waist, not the navel, unless your own protocol says otherwise.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
